Chapter: DetailPaneTest
  cites UI chapter DetailPane
  cites UI chapter StatusBadge
  cites UI chapter Dropdown
  cites UI chapter Widget
  cites UI chapter Theme

 The inspector pane TrackerApp, WorkflowHtml, CallPage and ItemDesignerApp
 all describe themselves as using. None of them renders one:
 `call-participant-detail`, `item-detail` and `wf-process-detail` are each
 mentioned exactly twice, at their signature and their definition, and
 neither `detail-widget` nor `detail-overlay` has a caller in `apps/`.
 Latent, and therefore unmeasured.

 The pane assembles itself out of four optional sections, so the shape it
 produces depends on which of them are present. Asking with all of them
 filled cannot tell a section that is conditional from one that is always
 emitted, so the empty spec is asked first and each section is added in
 turn.

 The badge is the field kind worth the most attention. `df-badge` takes a
 colour and all four real callers compute one to pass: CallPage derives one
 from mute, video and screen-share state, ItemDesignerApp calls
 `badge-label-color`, TrackerApp calls `tk-status-color`, WorkflowHtml calls
 `wf-status-badge-color`. Each now passes a TONE beside it and the render
 arm applies it.

 **The priority bar carries its COUNT now**, so Urgent and None no longer
 come out identical. `badge-widget` renders a gauge of bars against four
 rather than a fixed 12x12 custom node, which is the shape that already
 carries a quantity and that every renderer in the tree draws.

 This test asked that question before the fix and read `same=yes`, which is
 what it was built to pin. It went on reading `same=yes` for a moment after
 the fix too, because `payload` answered the bare word "gauge" for the one
 kind that carries numbers while every other arm printed its contents. A
 helper named for the payload has to print the payload; the category is
 never the assertion.

 We say:

Section: Reading

  at : WidgetNode, Integer -> WidgetNode
  at (w) (i) = list-at (w.wn-children) i

  payload : WidgetNode -> Text
  payload (w) = when w.wn-kind
    is WkLabel (t) -> "[" & t & "]"
    is WkButton (t) -> "<" & t & ">"
    is WkGauge (v) (m) -> "gauge(" & show v & "/" & show m & ")"
    is WkInput (t) (c) -> "input(" & t & ")"
    is WkSeparator -> "sep"
    is WkPanel -> "panel"
    is WkCustom (tag) -> "custom(" & tag & ")"

  kids : WidgetNode -> Text
  kids (w) = show (w.wn-child-count)

  ids : WidgetNode, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  ids (w) (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else let c = at w i
    in ids w (i + 1) n (acc & (if i > 0 then " " else "") & c.wn-id)

  shape : WidgetNode -> Text
  shape (w) = ids w 0 (w.wn-child-count) ""

Section: The specs

  bare : Integer -> DetailSpec
  bare (x) = detail-spec "dp" "Fix the thing" "TRK-42"

  with-fields : Integer -> DetailSpec
  with-fields (x) =
    let s = detail-spec "dp" "Fix the thing" "TRK-42"
    in let s1 = detail-add-field s (df-text "Owner" "ada")
    in let s2 = detail-add-field s1 (df-badge "Status" "Active" #F59E0B)
    in let s3 = detail-add-field s2 (df-dropdown "Cycle" (dropdown-with-selected "cyc" ["C1", "C2"] 1))
    in detail-add-field s3 (df-widget "Progress" (widget-gauge "pg" 3 10))

Section: Which sections appear

 A pane with nothing but a title is a header and a separator. Each
 optional section adds its own separator and heading, so the child count
 is what says whether a section was emitted at all.

  line-bare : Text =
    let w = detail-widget (bare 0)
    in "bare: kids=" & kids w & " shape=" & shape w

  line-fields : Text =
    let w = detail-widget (with-fields 0)
    in "fields: kids=" & kids w & " shape=" & shape w

  line-description : Text =
    let s = detail-set-description (bare 0) "A **bold** claim"
    in let w = detail-widget s
    in "description: kids=" & kids w & " shape=" & shape w

  line-activity : Text =
    let s = detail-add-activity (bare 0) "ada" "closed it" "09:41"
    in let w = detail-widget s
    in "activity: kids=" & kids w & " shape=" & shape w

  line-footer : Text =
    let s = detail-set-footer (bare 0) "3 attachments"
    in let w = detail-widget s
    in "footer: kids=" & kids w & " shape=" & shape w

Section: The header

 Subtitle, then title, then a close button. The order is printed rather
 than judged: an identifier ahead of a name is a real convention and this
 records what the pane does rather than what a reader expects.

  line-header : Text =
    let h = at (detail-widget (bare 0)) 0
    in "header: kids=" & kids h & " " & payload (at h 0) & payload (at h 1) & payload (at h 2)

Section: The four field kinds

 Each field is a row of a fixed-width label and a flexed value. What the
 value widget IS differs by kind, and that is the assertion.

  field-row : Integer -> WidgetNode
  field-row (i) = at (detail-widget (with-fields 0)) (i + 2)

  line-field-text : Text =
    let r = field-row 0
    in "text field: " & payload (at r 0) & payload (at r 1)

  line-field-badge : Text =
    let r = field-row 1
    in "badge field: " & payload (at r 0) & payload (at r 1)

  line-field-dropdown : Text =
    let r = field-row 2
    in "dropdown field: " & payload (at r 0) & payload (at r 1)

  line-field-widget : Text =
    let r = field-row 3
    in "widget field: " & payload (at r 0) & payload (at r 1)

 A badge and a text field are built from the same string here, so the only
 difference that can appear is the one being a badge is supposed to make.
 The size is printed as well as the payload, because the payload alone
 cannot see a size and a line blind to it would read as though the two
 were identical for a reason it had checked.

 **This line used to pin a defect and now pins a behaviour.** A plain
 `df-badge` still comes out identical to a text field, and that is correct:
 it names no tone, so it asks for none. What changed is that there is
 somewhere to put the meaning at all. `df-badge-toned` carries a tone, the
 render arm applies it, and the line below reads it back off the node.

 The pair is the point. The untoned badge staying identical to the text
 field is what stops the toned line passing for a reason it has not
 checked.

  boxed : WidgetNode -> Text
  boxed (w) = payload w & "@" & show (w.wn-min-w) & "x" & show (w.wn-min-h)

  line-badge-vs-text : Text =
    let s = detail-add-field (detail-add-field (detail-spec "cmp" "t" "s") (df-text "L" "Active")) (df-badge "L" "Active" #EF4444)
    in let w = detail-widget s
    in let a = at (at w 2) 1
    in let b = at (at w 3) 1
    in "badge vs text: text=" & boxed a & " badge=" & boxed b
    & " same-payload=" & (if payload a == payload b then "yes" else "no")
    & " untoned=" & tone-name (b.wn-tone)

  line-badge-toned : Text =
    let s = detail-add-field (detail-add-field (detail-spec "cmp" "t" "s") (df-badge "P" "Active" #EF4444)) (df-badge-toned "T" "Active" #EF4444 tone-error)
    in let w = detail-widget s
    in let plain = at (at w 2) 1
    in let toned = at (at w 3) 1
    in "badge tone: plain=" & tone-name (plain.wn-tone) & " toned=" & tone-name (toned.wn-tone)
    & " same-payload=" & (if payload plain == payload toned then "yes" else "no")

Section: What StatusBadge itself renders

 The same question one chapter over, because DetailPane is not where a
 badge is supposed to be drawn. Every BadgeStyle carries a colour and
 BsPriorityBar carries a bar count as well.

  line-badge-widget : Text =
    "badge-widget: urgent=" & payload (badge-widget "b1" (badge-priority 0))
    & " none=" & payload (badge-widget "b2" (badge-priority 4))
    & " same=" & (if payload (badge-widget "b1" (badge-priority 0)) == payload (badge-widget "b2" (badge-priority 4)) then "yes" else "no")

  line-badge-dots : Text =
    "badge dots: done=" & payload (badge-widget "b3" (badge-status 4))
    & " todo=" & payload (badge-widget "b4" (badge-status 1))
    & " pill-bug=" & payload (badge-widget "b5" (badge-label "bug"))
    & " pill-docs=" & payload (badge-widget "b6" (badge-label "docs"))

 The colours themselves are computed correctly and differ, which is what
 makes the rendering the loss rather than the source.

  line-badge-colors : Text =
    "colors differ: urgent=" & show (badge-priority-color 0)
    & " none=" & show (badge-priority-color 4)
    & " bug=" & show (badge-label-color "bug")
    & " docs=" & show (badge-label-color "docs")

Section: What a caller still holding the spec sees

 `detail-add-field` pushes onto the spec's own list and hands back the
 same record. The original is read AFTER the call, not snapshotted before.

  line-alias : Text =
    let s = detail-spec "al" "t" "s"
    in let added = detail-add-field s (df-text "Owner" "ada")
    in "alias: returned=" & show (list-length (added.ds-fields))
    & " original-after=" & show (list-length (s.ds-fields))

Section: Body

  report-a : Text =
    line-bare & "\n" & line-fields & "\n" & line-description & "\n"
    & line-activity & "\n" & line-footer & "\n" & line-header

  report-b : Text =
    line-field-text & "\n" & line-field-badge & "\n" & line-field-dropdown
    & "\n" & line-field-widget & "\n" & line-badge-vs-text

  report-c : Text =
    line-badge-toned & "\n" & line-badge-widget & "\n" & line-badge-dots & "\n" & line-badge-colors
    & "\n" & line-alias

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni report-a
    print-line-uni report-b
    print-line-uni report-c
  end
